@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

#
# prima-materia - the Hekhalot and Merkavah transmission
#
# Order of the file: the tradition, then the descent and the gate at which
# fitness is decided, then the capacity this literature states more plainly
# than any other the graph holds - knowledge of the Law obtained without
# study - and last the operative handbook with its numbers and its penalty.
# The instance was registered in traditions/registry-west-asia.ttl and is
# moved here now that passages have been read.
#
# The file is opened under the entrance SPEC section 14 gives priority to.
# What is claimed here can fail in public: a man who has performed the rite
# either can expound what is forbidden and permitted in an academy or cannot.
#

pmt:Hekhalot a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Hekhalot and Merkavah literature"@en, "Hekhalot- und Merkava-Literatur"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Jewish transmission of a descent through palaces whose gatekeepers test the descender, and of an adjuration by which knowledge of the Law is obtained without study."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered at corpus named until this file was written, and moved here on the first passages actually read. What was read at the wording: Hekhalot Rabbati, chapters 26, 30 and 31 in the Smith and Scholem translation edited by Karr, against the Hebrew of the Wertheimer recension; and the opening instructions of the Sword of Moses in Gaster's translation. Not opened: the Sar Torah paragraphs that carry the numbers, Maaseh Merkavah, Hekhalot Zutarti, Shiur Qomah, Sefer ha-Razim and 3 Enoch. The reasons stand in the issues named in the harvest note of prima-materia#ERNTE."@en ;
    skos:note "The paragraph numbering of this literature is Schäfer's, from an edition that could not be reached, and the translations read carry it in brackets. Where this file gives a chapter it is the chapter of the digital witness actually read, and where it gives a paragraph the paragraph is taken from that witness and not from the edition itself. That is a weaker citation than the rest of the graph uses and it is marked rather than smoothed. See prima-materia#PARAGRAPH."@en ;
    skos:note "How old the practice behind these texts is, and whether there is a practice behind them at all, is the open question of the field and is not decided here. The oldest manuscripts are of the thirteenth century and later, at least half a millennium after the composition anyone claims for the material. Two positions stand against each other and neither was read at the wording. See prima-materia#DATIERUNG."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Hekhalot Rabbati; Hekhalot Zutarti; the Sar Torah material; Maaseh Merkavah; Shiur Qomah; 3 Enoch; the Sword of Moses" ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapters 26, 30 and 31, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr, against the Hebrew of the Wertheimer recension" .

#
# The descent and the gate at which fitness is decided
#

pmp:YeridatMerkavah a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The descent to the Merkavah"@en, "Der Abstieg zur Merkava"@de ;
    skos:definition "The passage through the palaces, called a descent although the traveller goes up. The transmission does not explain the word anywhere in the passages read."@en ;
    skos:altLabel "yored merkavah"@en, "yordei merkavah"@en ;
    skos:note "The term is kept in the tradition's own form and not translated as ascent, because a rendering that corrected the direction would remove the only trace of whatever the word once meant. That it is nowhere explained is itself worth recording: a technical term that its own literature never glosses has usually come from somewhere else."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot ;
    dcterms:source "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapter 26, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr" .

pmc:HekhalotFitness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Fitness to descend to the Merkavah"@en, "Die Eignung zum Abstieg zur Merkava"@de ;
    skos:definition "The property the transmission holds to be decidable and to be decided at a particular place. The hashmal is said to divide, among those who descend, between those who were fit to descend and those who were not."@en ;
    skos:altLabel "hashmal"@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot ;
    dcterms:source "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapter 26, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr" .

pmc:SixthPalaceWaterTest a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "The water that is not there, at the gate of the sixth palace"@en, "Das Wasser, das keines ist, am Tor des sechsten Palastes"@de ;
    skos:definition "A protocol the transmission states for itself, complete in every part. The doorkeepers of the sixth palace place upon the descender a thousand thousands of waves of water, and there is not in that place even a single drop. The test is what he does next: the fault is not a wrong answer but the question. If he says what is the nature of this water, they run after him and stone him, saying that he is of the seed of them that kissed the calf and is unfit to see the King and his throne in his beauty; a voice goes out from the seventh heaven confirming the sentence; and he does not move from the spot before they place on him a thousand thousands of pieces of iron."@en ;
    skos:note "Silence is the pass and the question is the failure, which inverts the shape every other examination in this graph has. It also makes the test unusually cheap to administer and impossible to prepare for by learning an answer, since there is none."@en ;
    skos:note "What the protocol cannot survive is the same thing that undid the Maya examination in traditions/maya.ttl: the text that transmits the test also transmits the correct behaviour. From the moment this chapter is written down, the trial separates those who have read it from those who have not."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:HekhalotFitness ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A recorded case in which a descender held to be fit asked about the water, or in which one held unfit kept silent and passed. The transmission claims the gate decides fitness, so a single clean case on either side falls due against it." ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:source "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapter 26, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr" .

pmc:HekhalotDescentPresupposesFitness a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Passing the sixth palace presupposes fitness to descend"@en, "Das Bestehen am sechsten Palast setzt die Eignung zum Abstieg voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:YeridatMerkavah ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:HekhalotFitness ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The transmission states the consequence in a sequence and states it as violence, not as exclusion. The doorkeepers run after him and stone him; they name him of the seed of them that kissed the calf and unfit to see the King and his throne in his beauty; a voice from the seventh heaven confirms that they have said well; and he does not move from the spot before they place on him a thousand thousands of pieces of iron." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity because the consequence is quotable and because the passage sets no lesser outcome beside it: no second attempt, no purification that would restore the failed descender, no return."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapter 26, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr" .

pmc:WaterQuestionCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Do not ask about the water"@en, "Frage nicht nach dem Wasser"@de ;
    skos:definition "A caution whose object is a speech act and not a deed. What the descender must not do at the sixth palace is not touch, not turn back and not doubt, but ask; the transmission attaches the whole penalty to the question about the nature of the water."@en ;
    skos:note "A rule against asking is the form a tradition takes when the thing to be hidden is that there is nothing there. This is not offered as a reading of the passage's intent, which the graph cannot establish, but as the shape the rule has: the prohibition falls exactly on the act that would reveal the absence."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:YeridatMerkavah ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot ;
    dcterms:source "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapter 26, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr" .

#
# The capacity this literature states more plainly than any other the graph holds
#

pmc:SarTorah a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sar ha-Torah, the Prince of the Torah"@en, "Sar ha-Tora, der Fürst der Tora"@de ;
    skos:definition "The angelic power under whose name the Law is said to be obtained. The transmission gives its own reason for needing him: Israel's understanding was obscured by the exile and the words of the Law became hard as brass and iron, and what the world lacks is this secret and this mystery which is not in it. The remedy is offered as a means of loftiness by which the people are to raise themselves."@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Sar Torah"@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot ;
    dcterms:source "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapter 31, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr" .

pmc:TorahWithoutToil a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Learning the Law without toil"@en, "Das Erlernen des Gesetzes ohne Mühe"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity claimed. The transmission puts it as a contrast and not as a degree: the voice of the academies sounds, and not with toil and not with weariness do they learn, but with the name of this seal. What is promised beside it is public standing of a specific kind - to seat academies, to expound what is prohibited and what is permitted, to declare the impure impure and the pure pure, to determine the months and the intercalation of years, to appoint judges and to anoint patriarchs."@en ;
    skos:note "Set against it in the same passage is what the ordinary student must do without it, and the transmission puts that in the students' own prayer: may that which we have read remain in our possession, may that which we have studied remain in our possession. The claim is therefore not that learning is easier but that a different faculty is being offered in place of memory."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot ;
    dcterms:source "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapter 30, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr" .

pmc:SarTorahExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "The Law obtained without study"@en, "Das ohne Studium erlangte Gesetz"@de ;
    skos:definition "A protocol against the claim that the seal replaces study. The claim is unusually well placed for examination and the transmission itself supplies the reason: what it promises is not an inner state but a public performance before witnesses who can judge it. A man who sits in an academy and expounds what is forbidden and permitted either holds up under that or does not, and the people around him are competent to say which."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the rarest thing in the collection: a claim about an acquired capacity whose success condition the tradition itself locates outside the practitioner, in a room full of people qualified to contradict him. Most capacities in this graph are reported by the one who has them."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at procedure without cases, and the reason is that the passages carrying the numbers were not opened. The regime is said elsewhere in this literature to run forty days with immersions counted by the day, and to be annulled from the first day by a single lapse of purity; none of that was read at the wording here and none of it stands in this file. See prima-materia#REGIME."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:TorahWithoutToil ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A recorded case of a man who completed the rite as the transmission prescribes it and could not afterwards expound what is forbidden and permitted better than before, judged by the academy the transmission itself makes the judge. The claim is that toil is replaced, so an outcome indistinguishable from the untrained falls due against it." ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:source "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapters 30 and 31, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr" .

# --- ENDE ---
